 The shared sidebar projection hides rows whose durable Session summary has `origin: 'subagent'`; users enter those conversations through the selected parent's subagent header catalog. Each visible ordinary row inherits the blue activity indicator while any descendant reached through uninterrupted subagent-origin lineage is running, and its hover and assistive text report the exact running-descendant count without describing an idle parent as running. Ordinary forks remain visible and terminate this aggregation because lineage alone does not set their origin. Pending user interaction outranks the session's own running state, and either remains the primary row status while descendant activity stays available as a separate hover and assistive status. With neither present, descendant activity outranks the green unviewed-completion reminder; the reminder returns once no descendant is running. The runtime keeps hidden rows available for conversation, title, and addressed transport state.
 
+## Session grouping extension seam (experimental, v1alpha1)
+
+Third-party client plugins can contribute their own session grouping to this browser's view menu without replacing the sidebar slot. The seam is intentionally generic — grouping projections, bounded plain-text search terms, and session actions — and carries no domain types (no tags, folders, or favorites).
+
+A provider registers through the `ctx.sessionGroupings` service, which this package provides; registration and disposal belong to the calling plugin's fiber (unload/HMR clears the menu entry, subscription, and actions; duplicate ids fail loud):
+
+```ts
+import type { ClientContext } from '@deepseek-ai/dsh-client-runtime/client'
+import type { SessionGroupingProviderV1Alpha1 } from '@deepseek-ai/dsh-client-ui-workspace/client'
+
+export const inject = ['sessionGroupings'] as const
+
+export function apply(ctx: ClientContext): void {
+  const dispose = ctx.sessionGroupings.register({
+    id: 'org.example.my-grouping',           // unique; `workspace`/`flat`/`provider:` are reserved
+    label: () => 'By my thing',
+    getSnapshot: () => snapshot,             // stable reference until the next notify
+    subscribe: (listener) => subscribe(listener),
+    // optional: row actions appended to every session row menu
+    sessionActions: [{ id: 'org.example.edit', label: 'Edit…', open: (sessionId) => openEditor(sessionId) }],
+  })
+  // dispose() is idempotent and also runs when this plugin unloads.
+}
+```
+
+The provider owns group order, labels, and membership only. The browser keeps everything else: it filters unknown, archived, subagent-origin, and non-current blank sessions; renders native session rows (rename/fork/archive stay); keeps the current-session highlight on every copy of a multi-group session; applies the existing **Manual**/**Last updated** ordering per namespaced `provider:<providerId>:<groupId>` browser-local account (never a Workspace order account, never the Host reorder API); hides Workspace-only group-header actions (new/rename/delete/drag) on external groups; and merges each provider's `searchTermsBySession` into the local search while leaving the Host content-search bound unchanged. The persisted selection key for an external grouping is `provider:<providerId>`, so existing `workspace`/`flat` persisted values load unchanged and an unloaded provider falls back to the workspace view. Plugins must probe the capability first (`typeof ctx.sessionGroupings?.register === 'function'`) and degrade honestly on older hosts — no DOM patching, no sidebar replacement. The public surface is type-only (`SessionGroupingProviderV1Alpha1` and friends exported from `./client`); `fakeSessionGroupingProvider` in the same module doubles as a community sample (see `tests/grouping.client.spec.ts`).
+
 ## Model Experience
 
 None, as the picker is browser chrome; nothing here reaches a model request.
